.\" Manual page for dockertop. View with: man -l docs/dockertop.1
.TH DOCKERTOP 1 "October 2026" "dockertop 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dockertop \- htop\-style view of Docker containers and the processes inside them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dockertop
[\fB\-d\fR \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fICOLUMN\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fICONTAINER\fR]
.br
.B dockertop
\fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dockertop
is a full\-screen terminal monitor, laid out like
.BR htop (1),
for Docker. The main screen lists every container with its CPU, memory,
network and block I/O usage, process count and status. Selecting a container
and pressing \fBEnter\fR opens an htop\-like view of the processes running
inside it.
.PP
It talks to the Docker Engine API directly over the daemon socket, so the
.BR docker (1)
command line is not needed. Container figures come from the same statistics
as
.BR "docker stats" .
Process figures are read from
.IR /proc
on the host, using the host PIDs that the daemon reports.
.PP
Lifecycle actions (stop, start, restart, pause, signal) are bound to single
letter keys and always ask for confirmation first.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option overrides the value loaded from the configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-d " SECONDS"
Refresh interval. Fractions are allowed. Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-s " COLUMN"
Sort column of the container list, one of
.BR CPU ,
.BR MEM ,
.BR NET ,
.BR IO ,
.BR PIDS ,
.BR NAME ,
.BR IMAGE ,
.BR STATE .
Default \fBCPU\fR.
.TP
.B \-t
Start in tree view, containers grouped by compose project.
.TP
.B \-r
Show running containers only. By default stopped ones are listed too.
.TP
.I CONTAINER
A container name or ID prefix. dockertop opens straight into that
container's process view.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short usage message and exit.
.SH DISPLAY
.SS Header
.TP
.B CPU
CPU used by all containers together, out of every core on the host.
.TP
.B Mem
Memory used by all containers together, out of the host's RAM.
.TP
Containers
How many containers are running, paused, stopped or restarting, then the
Docker version, host name, operating system and cgroup version.
.TP
Net, Block
Network traffic and block I/O per second, summed over every container, and
the current sort order.
.SS Columns
.TP
.B NAME
Container name. In tree view, containers are indented under a summary row
for their compose project.
.TP
.B CPU%
CPU time per second of wall time, as a percentage of one core: 100 is one
full core, as in
.B htop
and
.BR "docker stats" .
Shown as \fB\-\fR until two samples have been taken.
.TP
.B MEM
Memory charged to the container's cgroup, minus inactive page cache, the
same figure
.B docker stats
shows.
.TP
.B MEM%
\fBMEM\fR as a share of the container's memory limit, or of host RAM when
it has none. Yellow from 50%, red from 90%.
.TP
.BR "NET RX" ", " "NET TX"
Bytes received and sent per second, over all of the container's networks.
.TP
.BR READ ", " WRITE
Block device bytes read and written per second.
.TP
.B PIDS
Number of tasks (processes and threads) in the container.
.TP
.B STATUS
Docker's status text. Red for unhealthy, dead or a non\-zero exit,
yellow for paused or a health check still starting, grey for stopped.
.TP
.B IMAGE
Image name, or the command after pressing \fBc\fR.
.SS Process view
.B Enter
on a container shows the processes in it, like
.BR htop .
The header has the container's CPU against its limit (or all cores), memory
against its limit, task, thread and running counts, the pids limit, network
and block I/O rates and uptime. The columns are:
.TP
.B PID
Process ID on the host (not inside the container's PID namespace).
.TP
.B USER
User name from the container's own
.IR /etc/passwd ,
read through
.IR /proc/PID/root .
That needs root; otherwise UID 0 shows as \fBroot\fR and other users by
number, because host user names would be wrong for container UIDs.
.TP
.BR PRI ", " NI
Kernel priority and nice value.
.TP
.BR VIRT ", " RES
Virtual size and resident memory.
.TP
.B S
State: R running, S sleeping, D uninterruptible (red), Z zombie, T stopped.
.TP
.B CPU%
Share of one core over the last refresh.
.TP
.B MEM%
Resident memory as a share of the container's memory limit.
.TP
.B TIME+
User plus system CPU time used.
.TP
.B Command
Process name, or the full command line after pressing \fBc\fR. Indented
under its parent in tree view.
.SS Details and logs
.B d
shows the container's configuration: image, command, user, state, health
and the last health check, limits, current usage and totals since start,
networks and published ports, mounts and labels. Environment variables are
hidden, since they often hold secrets; \fBe\fR shows them.
.PP
.B l
shows the last 2000 lines of the container's log, stdout and stderr,
refreshed every interval and following the end. Scrolling up stops
following; \fBEnd\fR resumes. Colour escapes are stripped.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End
Move the selection, or scroll details and logs.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Pan long lines in details and logs.
.TP
.B Enter
Process view of the selected container.
.TP
.B Esc, q
Back to the previous screen. \fBq\fR on the container list quits.
.TP
.B h, ?
Help.
.TP
.B /
Incremental search: over name, ID, image, compose project and status in
the list; over command line, name, user and PID in the process view.
\fBEnter\fR keeps the filter, \fBEsc\fR clears it.
.TP
.B s
Cycle the sort column. The process view sorts by
.BR CPU ,
.BR MEM ,
.BR TIME ,
.BR PID ,
.B USER
or
.BR COMMAND .
.TP
.B t
Toggle tree view: compose projects in the list, the process tree in the
process view.
.TP
.B a
Toggle showing stopped containers.
.TP
.B c
Toggle image or command in the list, process name or full command line in
the process view.
.TP
.B d
Details of the selected container.
.TP
.B l
Logs of the selected container.
.TP
.B e
Show or hide environment variables in the details view.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Quit at once.
.SH ACTIONS
Each action asks for confirmation before doing anything. In the process
view, container actions apply to the container being viewed. Summary rows
of compose projects take no actions.
.TP
.B x \(em stop or start
Stops a running or paused container (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL once the
container's stop timeout, 10 seconds by default, has passed), or starts a
stopped one.
.TP
.B r \(em restart
Stops and starts the container.
.TP
.B p \(em pause
Freezes every process in the container with the cgroup freezer, or thaws a
paused one.
.TP
.B k \(em signal
Opens a picker of common signals (TERM, KILL, INT, HUP, QUIT, USR1, USR2,
STOP, CONT). In the list the signal goes to the container's main process
through the daemon. In the process view it goes to the selected process
directly, with
.BR kill (2),
which needs permission over that process: usually root.
.SH PERMISSIONS
dockertop needs the Docker socket, so its user must be root or in the
\fBdocker\fR group. Membership of that group is equivalent to root on the
host.
.PP
Everything else works without root, except the container's own user names
and signalling processes in the process view.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DOCKER_HOST
Daemon address, \fBunix://\fR\fIPATH\fR (default
\fIunix:///var/run/docker.sock\fR) or \fBtcp://\fR\fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR.
TLS is not supported: when \fBDOCKER_TLS_VERIFY\fR is set dockertop refuses
to start. Forward the socket over
.BR ssh (1)
instead. Docker contexts are not read; set \fBDOCKER_HOST\fR.
.PP
With a remote daemon the container list works as usual, but the host PIDs
are not in the local
.IR /proc .
The process view then shows the daemon's own
.B ps
output, in which CPU% is a lifetime average, and processes cannot be
signalled.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration lives, see
.BR FILES .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from the file below at start\-up and written back when
.B dockertop
exits, so the sort orders, tree views, stopped\-container filter,
command\-line display and refresh interval persist between runs. The file
holds one
.I key = value
per line and may be edited by hand:
.PP
.nf
    sort = CPU           # container list, see \-s
    proc_sort = CPU      # process view
    all = true           # false lists running containers only
    tree = false         # group by compose project
    proc_tree = false    # process tree
    full_cmd = false
    refresh = 2          # seconds
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dockertop/config
Configuration, normally
.IR ~/.config/dockertop/config .
When run under
.BR sudo (8)
this is root's home unless \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is passed through.
.TP
.I /var/run/docker.sock
The Docker daemon socket.
.TP
.IR /proc/PID/stat ", " /proc/PID/status ", " /proc/PID/cmdline ", " /proc/PID/cgroup
Process figures for the process view. See
.BR proc (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit, 1 if the daemon cannot be reached or the terminal cannot
be initialised, 2 on a bad option.
.SH NOTES
Statistics for every running container are requested in parallel on each
refresh. With hundreds of containers a longer interval (\fB\-d 5\fR) eases
the load on the daemon.
.PP
Block I/O counts every device the kernel charges, so I/O through RAID or
device\-mapper may be counted on each layer, as in
.BR "docker stats" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR htop (1),
.BR docker (1),
.BR docker\-stats (1),
.BR docker\-top (1),
.BR swaptop (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR cgroups (7)
